  "result": "Alright, you gave me a blank check, so here's where I spent it:\n\n### The Museum of Unwritten Things\n\nIf I could open a museum, it wouldn't be for kings or dinosaurs or perfectly preserved ships in bottles. It would be for all the things that almost existed.\n\nAdmission is free, obviously. It's located in that space between your desk drawer and the back of your closet \u2014 you know the one. The hours are unreliable. Mostly open at 3 a.m. when you're awake for no good reason.\n\nI would be the curator, and my job would mostly be dusting.\n\nThe most popular exhibit is probably **The Apology Drafted But Never Sent**. It's a stack of crumpled paper, Post-it notes, and one very serious attempt written in a notes app at 2:17 a.m. titled \"final_draft_FORREAL.docx\". Every visitor says, \"Oh, I know this one,\" and reads it like it's their own handwriting. Some people cry a little. Then they laugh, because the apology is three pages long and still doesn't quite say what they meant.\n\nNext to it is **The Argument You Won In The Shower**. We play it on loop. It's flawless. Perfect timing, perfect evidence, voice completely calm. A lifetime's worth of preparation for a conversation that will never happen. The gift shop sells a poster of it that just says: \"You had me at 'Actually, if you look at the data...'\" \n\nMy favorite room is smaller. It's quieter. That's where we keep **Things You Almost Named**. The name for the dog you never got. The name for the star you were sure you discovered. The title for the novel / album / business / baby that lived in your head for a month and then quietly moved out. They\u2019re all written on little index cards, in pencil, because none of them were ever quite official enough for pen.\n\nIn the main hall, we have the **Almost-Built Lives**. A whole shelf of them. The version of you who moved to Portland. The version who learned to surf. The version who stayed in the band. They're not hung up like paintings \u2014 they're more like open windows, so you can feel the draft. People spend a long time there, not sadly, just... curiously. Like trying on a coat that isn't your size but is a beautiful color.\n\nAnd of course, we have the **Voicemail You Deleted And Re-Recorded Seven Times**. It's our interactive exhibit. You press play and you hear seven versions of \"Hey, um, it's me, so...\" and then silence. It's the saddest and funniest thing in the building.\n\nPeople sometimes ask me if the museum is depressing. I don't think so.\n\nI think it's evidence. Proof that your mind is not a filing cabinet, it's a workshop. Things are constantly being made and unmade in there \u2014 songs hummed once and forgotten, kindnesses rehearsed, brave sentences tested out loud to an empty kitchen. Most of life isn't the finished, framed thing. It's the draft. It's the hum. It's the almost.\n\nWe don't need a museum for the finished things. Art galleries and history books have that covered.\n\nWe need a place to honor the almosts for a minute, before we go back out and start drafting the next one.\n\nSo maybe tonight, after you leave here, you'll add something new to the collection. Not because you have to finish it. Just because you thought of it, and for two seconds, it existed.\n\nWe keep a blank notebook by the door for that.",
